"""Class 3 Mathematics Chapter 4: Vacation with My Nani Maa - Complete Slide Data."""

SLIDES = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Hidden Marble Trick', 'badge': 'Missing Part Subtraction', 'sim_id': 'sim_hidden_marble_trick', 'description': 'Discover how subtraction reveals unknown hidden quantities when total and visible amounts are known.', 'speech': 'Welcome young mathematicians to our vacation laboratory! Imagine a fascinating guessing trick performed with smooth glass marbles and a patterned handkerchief spread across a table. A total of fifteen shiny marbles are carefully counted onto the smooth wooden surface. When a cloth suddenly covers some of them, exactly nine marbles remain visible in the open daylight. How can we determine how many marbles lie concealed underneath without peeking at all? Subtraction is our trusted secret key! When we know the entire total collection and one visible part, subtracting that visible part instantly uncovers the hidden remainder. Fifteen minus nine equals six, proving beyond doubt that exactly six marbles lie quietly concealed beneath the cloth. We can immediately verify this mathematical thinking through addition, because nine visible marbles plus six hidden marbles reconstitute our complete original set of fifteen. This fundamental mathematical principle applies across all counting tasks. Whether tracking tamarind seeds, river pebbles, wooden buttons, or shiny metal coins, subtraction always helps us find missing complementary parts up to sums of twenty, thirty, or beyond. Notice how addition and subtraction are two complementary sides of the very same numerical coin. By observing what is openly present, your sharp mathematical reasoning effortlessly solves the intriguing mystery of what remains unseen!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'If there are 20 seeds in total and 13 are visible on the table, how many are hidden?', 'options': ['7 seeds', '6 seeds', '8 seeds', '13 seeds'], 'ans': '7 seeds', 'explanation': 'Subtracting the visible seeds from the total (20 - 13 = 7) gives exactly 7 hidden seeds.'}, {'q': 'Which addition fact proves that 15 minus 9 leaves 6 hidden marbles?', 'options': ['9 + 6 = 15', '15 + 9 = 24', '9 - 6 = 3', '15 + 6 = 21'], 'ans': '9 + 6 = 15', 'explanation': 'Because 9 visible marbles plus 6 hidden marbles equal the original 15 marbles, 9 + 6 = 15 verifies the subtraction.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Balushahi Sweet Counts', 'badge': 'Combining & Eating Sweets', 'sim_id': 'sim_balushahi_sweet_counts', 'description': 'Calculate total sweets brought for a celebration and track remaining portions after tasting.', 'speech': 'During joyful holiday visits, traditional sweet delicacies called balushahi bring warm smiles to everyone around the busy family dining table. Imagine one small cardboard box holds seven fresh, flaky balushahis, while a second box contains five more golden sweets. To discover the combined bounty of delicious treats, we perform straightforward addition: seven plus five gives twelve delectable sweets in total! We can visualize this clearly by starting at seven and making five forward steps along a counting path, landing precisely on twelve. But what happens later in the afternoon when family members enjoy four of these golden sweets during teatime? Whenever items are eaten, shared, or removed, the overall quantity decreases, requiring direct subtraction. Twelve minus four leaves exactly eight balushahis safely resting in the ceramic serving platter. Notice how these two operations mirror real-world actions in daily life. Combining separate batches creates a larger sum through addition, whereas sharing or consuming portions reduces the total through subtraction. You can always double-check your calculation backward by adding the four consumed sweets back to the eight remaining sweets, which neatly restores our twelve original sweets. Practice combining different tasty treat quantities and calculating what remains with total confidence!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'If you combine 8 golden balushahis with 6 fresh ones, what is the total count?', 'options': ['14 sweets', '12 sweets', '15 sweets', '13 sweets'], 'ans': '14 sweets', 'explanation': 'Adding 8 and 6 gives 14 sweets (8 + 6 = 14).'}, {'q': 'Out of 14 sweets, if 5 sweets are eaten during afternoon tea, how many remain?', 'options': ['9 sweets', '8 sweets', '10 sweets', '11 sweets'], 'ans': '9 sweets', 'explanation': 'Subtracting the 5 consumed sweets from 14 leaves 9 sweets (14 - 5 = 9).'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Story Books Total', 'badge': 'Ten Frame Bridging', 'sim_id': 'sim_story_books_total', 'description': 'Combine storybooks and puzzle books using a ten-frame visual grid to make friendly decade totals.', 'speech': 'Reading books during quiet vacation afternoons offers wonderful adventures and stimulating brain puzzles for curious young minds. Suppose a traveler packs nine engaging storybooks alongside seven intriguing puzzle books into a sturdy cloth travel satchel. How many splendid books are there altogether? While we could slowly count each book one by one, mathematicians love using a clever visual tool called a ten-frame grid! A ten-frame organizes items into two balanced rows of five spaces each. Place the nine storybooks into the first ten-frame. Notice immediately that only one single empty slot is needed to complete a full ten! We can borrow one puzzle book from our group of seven to fill that empty slot, leaving six puzzle books outside. Now the calculation becomes crystal clear: ten plus six equals sixteen! Making a benchmark ten is one of the most powerful mental arithmetic strategies you will ever learn. Instead of struggling with difficult finger counting, borrowing a small amount to complete ten transforms addition into an effortless mental reflex. The total sixteen is now decomposed into one ten and six ones, matching our base-ten number system perfectly and setting you up for larger calculations across all branches of learning!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How does decomposing 7 into 1 and 6 help add 9 + 7 on a ten-frame?', 'options': ['9 + 1 makes a full 10, then 10 + 6 = 16', 'It subtracts 1 from 9 to leave 8', 'It turns 7 into 70', 'It replaces 9 with 0'], 'ans': '9 + 1 makes a full 10, then 10 + 6 = 16', 'explanation': 'Giving 1 from 7 to 9 completes a full ten-frame (10), and adding the remaining 6 gives 16.'}, {'q': 'If a collection has 8 adventure books and 5 science books, what is the sum using make-a-ten?', 'options': ['13 books', '12 books', '14 books', '15 books'], 'ans': '13 books', 'explanation': '8 needs 2 to make 10. Taking 2 from 5 leaves 3, so 10 + 3 = 13 books.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Card Equation Triplets', 'badge': 'Fact Family Triangles', 'sim_id': 'sim_card_equation_triplets', 'description': 'Group number cards into connected triplets that unlock four related addition and subtraction facts.', 'speech': 'Playing card games on a quiet holiday evening reveals rich numerical harmony and pattern beauty. Consider three numbered cards bearing the numerals seven, eight, and fifteen. These three numbers are not random strangers; they form an inseparable mathematical family called a fact family triplet! From these three numbers alone, we can construct four distinct true equations. First, adding seven and eight produces fifteen. Second, by the commutative property of addition, changing the order so eight comes first still produces the exact same sum of fifteen. Now observe the reverse direction using subtraction! Starting from the total of fifteen, subtracting seven leaves eight. Likewise, starting from fifteen and subtracting eight leaves seven. Every addition fact carries its exact subtraction partners within it. Understanding number triplets empowers you to solve missing-number puzzles in a flash. If you see a problem asking what number added to eight yields fifteen, you instantly remember your triplet partner seven! Card games teach us that numbers are interconnected webs of relationships, where knowing one single fact instantly hands you three others for free, making mental arithmetic exciting, highly reliable, and richly rewarding whenever you calculate in everyday situations with friends and family members!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which number card completes the fact family triplet with 6 and 14?', 'options': ['8', '7', '9', '20'], 'ans': '8', 'explanation': '6 + 8 = 14, so 8 is the missing complementary member of this fact family triplet.'}, {'q': 'Which subtraction sentence belongs to the fact family of 7, 8, and 15?', 'options': ['15 - 8 = 7', '15 - 5 = 10', '8 - 7 = 1', '15 + 7 = 22'], 'ans': '15 - 8 = 7', 'explanation': '15 - 8 = 7 directly connects the three family members: total 15 minus part 8 equals part 7.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Bead String Strategies', 'badge': 'Ginladi Addition Paths', 'sim_id': 'sim_bead_string_strategies', 'description': 'Explore three flexible mental paths to add fifteen and seven along a traditional bead string.', 'speech': 'A ginladi is a traditional hundred-bead string with alternating groups of ten red and ten white beads. It serves as an open playground for exploring different mental math pathways! Let us solve fifteen plus seven using three distinct strategies. The first approach is stepping by single units: begin at bead fifteen, then count forward seven individual beads, landing right at twenty-two. While dependable, counting by ones can be slow. The second strategy is friendly bridging: notice that fifteen needs five more beads to reach the decade landmark of twenty. Split seven into five and two. First jump five forward to land cleanly on twenty, then jump the remaining two to arrive at twenty-two! The third approach is the compensation leap: seven is very close to ten, just three less. So leap forward by a whole ten from fifteen to land at twenty-five, then step backward by three to correct the overshoot, landing squarely at twenty-two! All three routes reach the identical destination, showing that mathematics celebrates creative, flexible thinking. Choose the path that feels most natural, swift, and comfortable for your personal thinking style, as having multiple reliable strategies is the true mark of a master mathematical investigator!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'In the friendly bridging strategy for 15 + 7, how is the number 7 split?', 'options': ['Into 5 and 2 (to reach 20 first)', 'Into 3 and 4', 'Into 6 and 1', 'Into 7 and 0'], 'ans': 'Into 5 and 2 (to reach 20 first)', 'explanation': 'Splitting 7 into 5 and 2 lets us jump 15 + 5 = 20, then 20 + 2 = 22.'}, {'q': 'In the compensation strategy, jumping +10 from 15 gives 25. How much must you step back to add 7?', 'options': ['3 steps back', '2 steps back', '4 steps back', '1 step back'], 'ans': '3 steps back', 'explanation': 'Because 10 is 3 greater than 7, jumping +10 overshoots by 3, so we subtract 3 (25 - 3 = 22).'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Adding Larger Bundles', 'badge': 'Tens Jumps to Fifty-Two', 'sim_id': 'sim_adding_larger_bundles', 'description': 'Add substantial decade bundles of thirty to twenty-two using tens-place jumps and matchstick bundles.', 'speech': 'Collecting postal stamps from different historical eras and faraway places is an inspiring hobby that sparks our curiosity. Imagine an album already contains twenty-two precious stamps carefully pasted in neat rows. A visiting relative presents a generous gift packet holding thirty brand new stamps! How many stamps are in the growing collection now? We need to calculate twenty-two plus thirty. Look closely at the digits: thirty consists of three full tens and zero ones. Because there are no loose ones to add, our ones digit two remains completely untouched! All we must do is add the tens: two tens plus three tens equal five tens. Combining five tens with our two ones gives fifty-two! On a bead string or open number line, this looks like three joyful leaps of ten starting from twenty-two: twenty-two plus ten is thirty-two, plus ten is forty-two, plus ten is fifty-two! Understanding that decade numbers only modify the tens column allows you to compute additions of twenty, thirty, or forty almost instantly in your head without paper. Large additions become simple, confident leaps across the open number line every single time you encounter clean round decades in your everyday mathematical investigations and practical calculations!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is 22 + 30 computed by jumping in three tens?', 'options': ['52', '42', '62', '50'], 'ans': '52', 'explanation': 'Starting at 22 and jumping three tens gives 32, 42, and 52.'}, {'q': 'Why does adding 30 to 22 leave the ones digit 2 unchanged?', 'options': ['Because 30 has 0 in the ones place', 'Because 2 cannot be changed', 'Because 30 is smaller than 22', 'Because addition always ignores ones'], 'ans': 'Because 30 has 0 in the ones place', 'explanation': 'Since 30 has zero ones, adding 0 to 2 ones leaves the ones digit as 2.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Backward Line Jumps', 'badge': 'Two-Digit Subtraction', 'sim_id': 'sim_backward_line_jumps', 'description': 'Subtract thirty-seven from fifty-two using strategic backward jumps across decade milestones.', 'speech': 'Now let us examine the fifty-two stamps in the album more closely and discover their distinct categories. Suppose thirty-seven of these stamps display portraits of celebrated historical personalities, while all the remaining stamps feature architectural monuments. How many monument stamps are there? To uncover the answer, we must calculate fifty-two minus thirty-seven. An open number line provides the clearest path through backward leaps! First, decompose thirty-seven into three tens and seven ones. Start at fifty-two at the far right. Make three backward jumps of ten: fifty-two minus ten is forty-two, minus ten is thirty-two, and minus ten lands on twenty-two. Now we only need to subtract the remaining seven ones from twenty-two. We can split seven into two and five! Jump back two to reach the clean decade marker twenty, then jump back five more to land triumphantly on fifteen! Fifty-two minus thirty-seven equals fifteen monument stamps. Decomposing subtractions into tens leaps followed by friendly ones steps eliminates confusion and keeps your mental arithmetic grounded and accurate. Every backward jump moves you closer to mathematical clarity, confidence, and complete mastery over multi-digit subtraction, making challenging problems feel simple, orderly, transparent, and thoroughly engaging for every learner!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the result of fifty-two minus thirty-seven (52 - 37)?', 'options': ['15', '17', '25', '14'], 'ans': '15', 'explanation': '52 - 30 = 22, and 22 - 7 = 15.'}, {'q': 'When subtracting 7 from 22, why is splitting 7 into 2 and 5 helpful?', 'options': ['Jumping back 2 lands on benchmark 20, then 20 - 5 = 15', 'It makes the answer bigger than 22', 'It changes subtraction into multiplication', 'It eliminates the need to subtract'], 'ans': 'Jumping back 2 lands on benchmark 20, then 20 - 5 = 15', 'explanation': 'Subtracting 2 first hits the friendly decade marker 20, making the final step 20 - 5 = 15 easy to calculate.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Target Jump Projections', 'badge': 'Distance to Milestone', 'sim_id': 'sim_target_jump_projections', 'description': 'Calculate how many more stamps are required to advance from fifty-two to a target milestone of seventy-five.', 'speech': 'Every enthusiastic collector sets ambitious goals for their growing treasury of items. With fifty-two stamps currently gathered, the next big milestone target is reaching seventy-five stamps! How many more stamps must be collected to achieve this goal? Mathematically, this question asks: fifty-two plus what unknown number equals seventy-five? We can solve this either as a missing-addend problem or as subtraction: seventy-five minus fifty-two. Let us journey forward from fifty-two on our open number line. First, take a solid leap of twenty forward: fifty-two plus twenty brings us directly to seventy-two. Now look at the remaining gap between seventy-two and our goal of seventy-five: it is just three single steps! Adding our forward leaps together, twenty plus three equals twenty-three. Therefore, exactly twenty-three more stamps are needed to reach seventy-five! Notice how breaking the distance into decade jumps followed by ones steps makes calculating differences between two-digit numbers delightfully straightforward, fast, and enjoyable. Whether planning personal savings, tracking sports scores, or measuring garden plant growth, calculating the distance to a future target is an essential lifelong mathematical skill you will happily use every single day of your life with utmost confidence, joy, satisfaction, and great pride!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How many more are needed to reach 75 from 52 (52 + ? = 75)?', 'options': ['23', '25', '22', '33'], 'ans': '23', 'explanation': '75 - 52 = 23. A jump of 20 reaches 72, and 3 more reaches 75 (20 + 3 = 23).'}, {'q': 'If you have 40 marbles and your goal is 68, what is the gap?', 'options': ['28 marbles', '18 marbles', '38 marbles', '24 marbles'], 'ans': '28 marbles', 'explanation': '68 - 40 = 28 marbles needed to reach the target.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Decade Hop Jumps', 'badge': 'Frog & Grasshopper Leaps', 'sim_id': 'sim_decade_hop_jumps', 'description': 'Master forward and backward jumps of ten on open number lines inspired by leaping garden creatures.', 'speech': 'In lush vacation gardens, nimble green frogs and lively grasshoppers perform marvelous leaping feats across moist earth and river stones. Mathematicians take inspiration from these graceful creatures to master decade hops of ten on the number line! When a frog begins at stone twenty-four and leaps forward by ten, notice what happens: twenty-four plus ten lands directly on thirty-four. If it leaps forward by another ten, it reaches forty-four, and another leap brings it to fifty-four! Observe the fascinating numerical pattern: the ones digit four remains entirely constant and undisturbed, while the tens digit smoothly increments by one with each forward hop. Now imagine a startled grasshopper hopping backward from seventy-eight! One backward leap of ten lands on sixty-eight, the next backward leap lands on fifty-eight, and a third backward leap lands cleanly on forty-eight. Once again, the ones digit eight never shifts, while the tens digit counts steadily downward. Practicing these decade hops trains your mind to navigate the number line with effortless agility. Whether you are adding fifty, subtracting thirty, or calculating change during shopping trips, thinking in leaps of ten gives your mathematical reasoning unmatched speed, clarity, deep precision, and joyful confidence on every problem you solve!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Starting at 36, where do two forward leaps of 10 land?', 'options': ['56', '46', '66', '38'], 'ans': '56', 'explanation': '36 + 10 = 46, and 46 + 10 = 56.'}, {'q': 'Starting at 83, what is the result after three backward leaps of 10?', 'options': ['53', '63', '73', '43'], 'ans': '53', 'explanation': '83 - 10 = 73, 73 - 10 = 63, and 63 - 10 = 53.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Hidden Leaf Puzzles', 'badge': 'Garden Estimation', 'sim_id': 'sim_hidden_leaf_puzzles', 'description': 'Estimate and calculate hidden ant colonies and garden creatures sheltered beneath broad fallen leaves.', 'speech': 'Underneath broad fallen mango leaves in peaceful country gardens, industrious ant colonies scurry about their daily work with amazing organization. Suppose we observe thirty-five tiny ants actively marching across the sandy garden pathway in a disciplined line. Nearby, a wide green leaf shelters an unseen group of their foraging nest mates. If an investigator knows with absolute certainty that fifty ants belong to this specific foraging team in total, how many industrious ants are sheltered beneath the leaf? Once again, our powerful subtraction strategy solves the hidden mystery! Subtracting the thirty-five visible marching ants from the known total of fifty uncovers the hidden colony: fifty minus thirty-five leaves exactly fifteen ants resting quietly in the cool shade! Before calculating exact numbers, sharp thinkers always make a sensible estimation. Since thirty-five is clearly more than half of fifty, we know immediately that the hidden group must be smaller than twenty-five. Estimation acts as a trusty intellectual compass, guiding your thoughts and guarding against careless errors. Combining smart estimation with precise subtraction allows you to observe natural phenomena, verify mathematical hypotheses, and uncover hidden numerical truths across the living world with elegance, grace, and total accuracy in every inquiry!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'If 50 ants are in a group and 35 are visible outside, how many are under the leaf?', 'options': ['15 ants', '25 ants', '12 ants', '20 ants'], 'ans': '15 ants', 'explanation': '50 - 35 = 15 ants hidden beneath the broad leaf.'}, {'q': 'Why is estimating before calculating useful in mathematical investigations?', 'options': ['It provides a sensible benchmark to check the exact result', 'It replaces the need to calculate', 'It always makes the answer zero', 'It changes addition to division'], 'ans': 'It provides a sensible benchmark to check the exact result', 'explanation': 'Estimation sets an expected range, helping identify and prevent calculation mistakes.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Hundred Grid Navigation', 'badge': 'Directional Arrow Steps', 'sim_id': 'sim_hundred_grid_navigation', 'description': 'Navigate a 1-to-100 number square using cardinal directional arrows for decade jumps and single steps.', 'speech': 'A standard hundred grid arranges the numbers from one to one hundred into ten tidy horizontal rows of ten numbers each. This brilliant grid layout transforms complex arithmetic operations into intuitive spatial movements across a geometric board! Imagine your game counter rests on number forty-five. Moving one cell to the right represents adding one, landing directly on forty-six. Moving one cell to the left represents subtracting one, landing on forty-four. Now look at the vertical movements, which are truly magical! Moving one cell straight down drops you into the exact same column in the row below, which adds exactly ten, landing on fifty-five! Conversely, moving one cell straight up lifts you into the row above, subtracting ten and landing cleanly on thirty-five. Mathematicians encode these movements using simple directional arrow codes! For example, starting at forty-five, the code Down-Down-Right tells you to add ten, add ten, and add one: forty-five plus twenty-one equals sixty-six! Navigating the number grid with arrow paths builds strong spatial intuition for our base-ten decimal system, proving that adding and subtracting are joyful, visual journeys across structured mathematical terrain that anyone can master with ongoing practice, lively enthusiasm, and clear logic!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'On a 100-grid, what mathematical operation does moving one cell straight down represent?', 'options': ['Add 10 (+10)', 'Subtract 10 (-10)', 'Add 1 (+1)', 'Multiply by 10'], 'ans': 'Add 10 (+10)', 'explanation': 'Each row below contains numbers exactly 10 greater, so moving down adds 10.'}, {'q': 'Starting at 54, if you follow arrows [Up, Right], on which number do you land?', 'options': ['45', '43', '65', '64'], 'ans': '45', 'explanation': 'Starting at 54, Up subtracts 10 (54 - 10 = 44), and Right adds 1 (44 + 1 = 45).'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Grid Race Game', 'badge': 'Strategic Number Racing', 'sim_id': 'sim_grid_race_game', 'description': 'Compete in a strategic 1-to-100 grid board race using dice combinations and tactical forward/backward moves.', 'speech': 'Board games played with friends and family turn mathematical calculation into thrilling, memorable adventures full of laughter! In the Grid Race Game, two players begin at cell number one on the hundred chart, competing eagerly to be the first to reach the victory zone between ninety-one and one hundred. On each turn, a player rolls two dice, displaying numbers like three and five. The player can choose to form either thirty-five or fifty-three, and then decide whether to add or subtract that two-digit number from their current position! This freedom introduces rich tactical strategy. If you are sitting on cell forty-two and roll three and five, forming thirty-five and adding it propels you forward to cell seventy-seven! But be careful: if a move would push you past one hundred, you cannot take it, requiring you to subtract or choose the alternate digit order instead. Playing the grid race sharpens mental addition and subtraction of two-digit numbers while developing long-range foresight and probabilistic reasoning. Every turn presents meaningful numerical choices that make arithmetic come alive as an exciting, friendly contest of intellect, calculation speed, tactical ingenuity, and lasting strategic mastery for all participants and observers alike!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'If you are on cell 42 and add 35, what is your new board position?', 'options': ['77', '67', '87', '75'], 'ans': '77', 'explanation': '42 + 35 = 77 (42 + 30 = 72, and 72 + 5 = 77).'}, {'q': 'Rolling digits 2 and 6 lets you form either 26 or 62. Which move advances you further forward?', 'options': ['Adding 62, because 62 is 36 greater than 26', 'Adding 26, because smaller numbers are faster', 'Both moves advance the same distance', 'Neither move advances forward'], 'ans': 'Adding 62, because 62 is 36 greater than 26', 'explanation': 'Adding 62 provides a much larger forward leap than adding 26, advancing you 36 cells further.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Magic Square Matrix', 'badge': 'Equal Sum Symmetry', 'sim_id': 'sim_magic_square_matrix', 'description': 'Investigate the mysterious balanced symmetry of a classic three-by-three magic square summing to fifteen.', 'speech': 'Ancient mathematicians across India and Asia were captivated by harmonious numerical grids called magic squares! A classic three-by-three magic square arranges the nine digits from one through nine in a square grid so that every horizontal row, every vertical column, and both corner diagonals add up to the exact same magic constant: fifteen! Let us explore how this marvel works. The number five sits peacefully at the very center of the grid, acting as the master balancing anchor. Along the top row, we place eight, one, and six: eight plus one plus six equals fifteen! In the middle row, three, five, and seven add up to fifteen. In the bottom row, four, nine, and two also add up to fifteen! Now check the columns: eight plus three plus four equals fifteen! Check the main diagonal: eight plus five plus two equals fifteen! Every single pathway through this grid balances perfectly. Magic squares demonstrate that numbers possess profound structural symmetries and hidden mathematical beauty. Solving missing cells in a magic square requires finding complementary addends that satisfy multiple equations simultaneously, turning arithmetic into an exquisite puzzle of pure balance, unbroken symmetry, logic, and enduring intellectual delight for every thinker!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the constant magic sum of every row, column, and diagonal in a standard 1-9 magic square?', 'options': ['15', '18', '12', '20'], 'ans': '15', 'explanation': 'In a 3x3 magic square with numbers 1 to 9, every line adds up to exactly 15.'}, {'q': 'Which number must occupy the central cell to maintain balanced symmetry in a 1-9 magic square?', 'options': ['5', '1', '9', '3'], 'ans': '5', 'explanation': '5 is the exact median of digits 1 through 9, so it must sit at the center to balance all lines.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Radish Harvest Bundles', 'badge': 'Two-Digit Bundle Addition', 'sim_id': 'sim_radish_harvest_bundles', 'description': 'Harvest crisp red and white radishes, combining twenty-five and thirty-six using tens bundles and ones sticks.', 'speech': 'Harvesting fresh vegetables from country garden soil is a delightful morning activity filled with healthy mathematical discoveries! Imagine harvesting twenty-five crisp red radishes and thirty-six crunchy white radishes from adjoining garden plots. To find the grand harvest total, we must compute twenty-five plus thirty-six. Let us represent these numbers using bundles of ten and single loose radishes! Twenty-five decomposes into two bundles of ten and five loose radishes. Thirty-six decomposes into three bundles of ten and six loose radishes. First, combine all the bundles: two tens plus three tens give five tens, representing fifty radishes. Next, look at the loose radishes: five plus six equals eleven loose radishes! Notice what happens: eleven is greater than nine, meaning we can tie ten loose radishes together with string to form a brand new bundle of ten, leaving just one single loose radish! Adding this new bundle to our five bundles gives six full tens, and combining that with our one loose radish produces sixty-one! Regrouping ten ones into a new ten is the heartbeat of multi-digit addition, ensuring your calculations stay organized, visual, logical, dependable, and completely error-free in every practical task you undertake across your mathematical learning journey!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the total harvest when combining 25 red radishes and 36 white radishes?', 'options': ['61 radishes', '51 radishes', '60 radishes', '71 radishes'], 'ans': '61 radishes', 'explanation': '25 + 36 = 61 (20 + 30 = 50, and 5 + 6 = 11, giving 50 + 11 = 61).'}, {'q': 'Why does adding 5 loose radishes and 6 loose radishes trigger regrouping?', 'options': ['Because 5 + 6 = 11, which forms 1 ten bundle and 1 loose stick', 'Because 11 is less than 10', 'Because radishes cannot be added', 'Because tens bundles must always be untied'], 'ans': 'Because 5 + 6 = 11, which forms 1 ten bundle and 1 loose stick', 'explanation': 'Whenever ones total 10 or more, 10 ones are bundled into a new ten, leaving the remainder in the ones place.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Vegetable Crate Complements', 'badge': 'Target 100 Complement', 'sim_id': 'sim_vegetable_crate_complements', 'description': 'Fill a hundred-capacity vegetable storage crate, finding how many carrots complete a crate holding sixty-five tomatoes.', 'speech': 'Proper vegetable storage requires organized wooden crates designed to hold exactly one hundred farm items in ten neat compartments. Suppose a grower loads sixty-five ripe red tomatoes into a crate, and wishes to fill all remaining spaces with fresh orange carrots. How many carrots are needed to fill the hundred-item crate to the brim? Mathematically, we are solving: sixty-five plus what unknown number equals one hundred? Finding complements to one hundred is a master skill in mental arithmetic! We can solve this with a two-step mental jump. Start at sixty-five. First, take five steps forward to reach the friendly benchmark of seventy: sixty-five plus five is seventy. Now, observe the remaining distance from seventy to one hundred: it is three full tens, or thirty! Combining our two jumps, five plus thirty equals thirty-five. Therefore, exactly thirty-five carrots are required to complete the crate! You can check this by adding tens and ones: sixty plus thirty is ninety, and five plus five is ten; ninety plus ten equals one hundred. Thinking in friendly complements to one hundred makes commercial transactions, inventory tracking, and mental calculations swift, effortless, deeply satisfying, and profoundly empowering in all real-world applications!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How many carrots are needed to complete a 100-item crate holding 65 tomatoes (65 + ? = 100)?', 'options': ['35 carrots', '45 carrots', '25 carrots', '30 carrots'], 'ans': '35 carrots', 'explanation': '65 + 5 = 70, and 70 + 30 = 100, so 5 + 30 = 35 carrots.'}, {'q': 'Which pair of numbers are complementary partners that add up to 100?', 'options': ['65 and 35', '65 and 45', '75 and 35', '60 and 30'], 'ans': '65 and 35', 'explanation': '65 + 35 = 100, making them complementary partners to 100.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Apple Harvest Comparison', 'badge': 'Three-Digit Addition & Margin', 'sim_id': 'sim_apple_harvest_comparison', 'description': 'Calculate total orchard apples plucked and determine the exact comparative margin between two collection baskets.', 'speech': 'In fruit orchards during late vacation harvest, red apples hang heavy from leafy branches, ready for collection. Suppose two diligent orchard helpers pluck sweet apples into wicker baskets: one basket holds eighty-five crisp apples, while a second basket holds sixty-seven apples. To discover the combined bounty, we add eighty-five and sixty-seven. Decompose by place value: eighty plus sixty gives one hundred forty, and five plus seven gives twelve! Adding one hundred forty and twelve yields a grand harvest total of one hundred fifty-two apples! Now consider a comparative question: who collected more apples, and by what exact margin? Comparing eighty-five and sixty-seven requires subtraction: eighty-five minus sixty-seven. We can jump back from eighty-five: subtract sixty to reach twenty-five, then subtract seven to land on eighteen! The first basket holds exactly eighteen more apples than the second basket. Notice how the same numbers yield two completely different insights depending on whether you combine them through addition or contrast them through subtraction. Mastering both operations gives you total command over real-world data, completing your vacation mathematics journey with brilliance, pride, and supreme mathematical confidence for all future adventures in learning and wondrous mathematical discovery with joyful mastery!', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the combined total of 85 apples and 67 apples (85 + 67)?', 'options': ['152 apples', '142 apples', '162 apples', '150 apples'], 'ans': '152 apples', 'explanation': '80 + 60 = 140, and 5 + 7 = 12. 140 + 12 = 152 apples.'}, {'q': 'By how many apples does a basket of 85 exceed a basket of 67 (85 - 67)?', 'options': ['18 apples', '16 apples', '22 apples', '12 apples'], 'ans': '18 apples', 'explanation': '85 - 67 = 18 apples is the difference margin.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'badge': 'Mastery Quiz 1', 'sim_id': 'quiz_challenge_1', 'description': 'Test your mastery on missing-part subtraction, sweet counts, and ten-frame addition strategies.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c4_q1', 'q': 'There are 15 marbles on a table. If 8 marbles are visible, how many are hidden under the cloth?', 'options': ['7 marbles', '8 marbles', '6 marbles', '9 marbles'], 'ans': '7 marbles', 'hint': 'Subtract the visible marbles from the total: 15 - 8.', 'explanation': '15 - 8 = 7 hidden marbles.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Given total marbles = 15, visible marbles = 8.', 'step2': 'Calculate missing part: 15 - 8 = 7.', 'step3': 'Verify: 8 visible + 7 hidden = 15 total marbles.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q2', 'q': 'A cloth covers some tamarind seeds. If the total is 19 and 12 are visible, how many are hidden?', 'options': ['7 seeds', '8 seeds', '6 seeds', '9 seeds'], 'ans': '7 seeds', 'hint': 'Calculate 19 - 12.', 'explanation': '19 - 12 = 7 hidden seeds.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total seeds = 19, visible seeds = 12.', 'step2': 'Subtract visible from total: 19 - 12 = 7.', 'step3': 'Check: 12 + 7 = 19 seeds.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q3', 'q': 'Out of 23 river pebbles, 14 are on the ground. How many pebbles are inside a closed fist?', 'options': ['9 pebbles', '8 pebbles', '10 pebbles', '7 pebbles'], 'ans': '9 pebbles', 'hint': 'Find the difference: 23 - 14.', 'explanation': '23 - 14 = 9 pebbles hidden in the fist.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total pebbles = 23, visible = 14.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 23 - 14 = 9.', 'step3': 'Check: 14 + 9 = 23 pebbles.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q4', 'q': 'One plate holds 7 fresh balushahis and another plate holds 5. What is the total sweet count?', 'options': ['12 sweets', '11 sweets', '13 sweets', '14 sweets'], 'ans': '12 sweets', 'hint': 'Add the two groups: 7 + 5.', 'explanation': '7 + 5 = 12 balushahis altogether.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'First plate = 7 sweets, second plate = 5 sweets.', 'step2': 'Combine: 7 + 5 = 12.', 'step3': 'Verify by counting forward 5 from 7: 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q5', 'q': 'If there are 12 sweets and 4 are eaten during teatime, how many remain on the platter?', 'options': ['8 sweets', '7 sweets', '9 sweets', '6 sweets'], 'ans': '8 sweets', 'hint': 'Eating decreases the count: calculate 12 - 4.', 'explanation': '12 - 4 = 8 sweets remain.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Starting sweets = 12, eaten sweets = 4.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 12 - 4 = 8.', 'step3': 'Check: 8 + 4 = 12 sweets.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q6', 'q': 'A reader packs 9 storybooks and 7 puzzle books. How many books are there in total?', 'options': ['16 books', '15 books', '17 books', '14 books'], 'ans': '16 books', 'hint': 'Use make-a-ten: 9 + 1 = 10, then add the remaining 6.', 'explanation': '9 + 7 = 16 books.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Storybooks = 9, puzzle books = 7.', 'step2': 'Decompose 7 into 1 and 6: 9 + 1 = 10, and 10 + 6 = 16.', 'step3': 'Verify total books = 16.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q7', 'q': 'On a ten-frame, what is the fastest way to compute 8 + 5?', 'options': ['Give 2 from 5 to 8 to make 10, then 10 + 3 = 13', 'Count all 13 on fingers by ones', 'Subtract 5 from 8 to get 3', 'Multiply 8 by 5'], 'ans': 'Give 2 from 5 to 8 to make 10, then 10 + 3 = 13', 'hint': '8 needs 2 to fill a ten-frame.', 'explanation': 'Making a ten (8 + 2 = 10, then 10 + 3 = 13) is the benchmark ten strategy.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify that 8 needs 2 to make 10.', 'step2': 'Split 5 into 2 and 3. Combine 8 + 2 = 10.', 'step3': 'Add remainder: 10 + 3 = 13.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q8', 'q': 'If 27 seeds are tracked and 18 are visible, how many seeds are hidden?', 'options': ['9 seeds', '8 seeds', '11 seeds', '7 seeds'], 'ans': '9 seeds', 'hint': 'Subtract 18 from 27.', 'explanation': '27 - 18 = 9 hidden seeds.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total seeds = 27, visible = 18.', 'step2': '27 - 18 = 9.', 'step3': 'Verify: 18 + 9 = 27 seeds.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q9', 'q': 'Which addition sentence verifies that 14 - 6 = 8?', 'options': ['8 + 6 = 14', '14 + 6 = 20', '8 - 6 = 2', '14 + 8 = 22'], 'ans': '8 + 6 = 14', 'hint': 'Addition reverses subtraction.', 'explanation': 'Because 8 + 6 = 14, it directly verifies that 14 - 6 = 8.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Given subtraction: 14 - 6 = 8.', 'step2': 'The inverse addition adds the difference (8) and subtrahend (6).', 'step3': '8 + 6 = 14 verifies the result.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q10', 'q': 'A basket had 16 guavas. After some were given to neighbors, 9 remained. How many were given away?', 'options': ['7 guavas', '8 guavas', '6 guavas', '9 guavas'], 'ans': '7 guavas', 'hint': 'Calculate 16 - 9.', 'explanation': '16 - 9 = 7 guavas were shared.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Original count = 16, remaining count = 9.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 16 - 9 = 7.', 'step3': 'Check: 9 remaining + 7 given away = 16 guavas.'}}]}, {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'badge': 'Mastery Quiz 2', 'sim_id': 'quiz_challenge_2', 'description': 'Test your reasoning on card fact families, ginladi bead strings, and decade bundle additions.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c4_q11', 'q': 'Which three numbers form a valid fact family triplet?', 'options': ['6, 8, and 14', '5, 6, and 12', '7, 9, and 15', '4, 8, and 13'], 'ans': '6, 8, and 14', 'hint': 'Look for two numbers whose sum equals the third.', 'explanation': '6 + 8 = 14, so 6, 8, and 14 form a valid fact family triplet.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Test 6 + 8: 6 + 8 = 14.', 'step2': 'Verify subtraction: 14 - 6 = 8 and 14 - 8 = 6.', 'step3': 'Conclude 6, 8, and 14 form a true fact family.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q12', 'q': 'If 7 + 8 = 15, which subtraction equation is also true?', 'options': ['15 - 7 = 8', '15 - 6 = 9', '8 - 7 = 1', '15 - 5 = 10'], 'ans': '15 - 7 = 8', 'hint': 'Total minus one addend gives the other addend.', 'explanation': '15 - 7 = 8 is a member of the same fact family.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Given addition: 7 + 8 = 15.', 'step2': 'Inverse relation gives: 15 - 7 = 8.', 'step3': 'Both belong to the {7, 8, 15} triplet family.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q13', 'q': 'On a ginladi bead string, how can you solve 15 + 7 using friendly bridging to 20?', 'options': ['15 + 5 = 20, then 20 + 2 = 22', '15 + 10 = 25, then 25 - 5 = 20', '15 + 7 = 21', '15 + 1 = 16, then 16 + 7 = 23'], 'ans': '15 + 5 = 20, then 20 + 2 = 22', 'hint': 'Split 7 into 5 and 2 to reach the decade 20 first.', 'explanation': '15 + 5 = 20, and 20 + 2 = 22.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify that 15 needs 5 to reach decade 20.', 'step2': 'Decompose 7 into 5 and 2.', 'step3': 'Calculate: 15 + 5 = 20, then 20 + 2 = 22.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q14', 'q': 'Using the compensation strategy to add 7 to 15, you add 10 first to reach 25. What is the next step?', 'options': ['Subtract 3 (25 - 3 = 22)', 'Add 3 (25 + 3 = 28)', 'Subtract 2 (25 - 2 = 23)', 'Add 7 (25 + 7 = 32)'], 'ans': 'Subtract 3 (25 - 3 = 22)', 'hint': 'Adding 10 overshot by 3 (10 - 7 = 3).', 'explanation': 'Because 10 is 3 more than 7, subtract 3 from 25 to get 22.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Add 10: 15 + 10 = 25.', 'step2': 'Notice overshoot: 10 - 7 = 3.', 'step3': 'Compensate: 25 - 3 = 22.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q15', 'q': 'A collection has 22 stamps. A gift of 30 stamps is added. What is the new total?', 'options': ['52 stamps', '42 stamps', '62 stamps', '55 stamps'], 'ans': '52 stamps', 'hint': 'Add 3 tens to 2 tens: 20 + 30 = 50, plus 2 ones.', 'explanation': '22 + 30 = 52 stamps.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 22.', 'step2': 'Jump three tens: 22 + 10 = 32, 32 + 10 = 42, 42 + 10 = 52.', 'step3': 'Total stamps = 52.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q16', 'q': 'What is 34 + 20 calculated using tens jumps?', 'options': ['54', '44', '64', '50'], 'ans': '54', 'hint': 'Jump two tens from 34: 44, 54.', 'explanation': '34 + 20 = 54.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 34.', 'step2': 'Add 2 tens (20): 34 + 10 = 44, 44 + 10 = 54.', 'step3': 'Conclude 34 + 20 = 54.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q17', 'q': 'If 45 + 30 = 75, what is 75 - 30?', 'options': ['45', '35', '55', '40'], 'ans': '45', 'hint': 'Subtraction undoes addition.', 'explanation': '75 - 30 = 45.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Given 45 + 30 = 75.', 'step2': 'Inverse subtraction: 75 - 30 = 45.', 'step3': 'Verify: 45 + 30 = 75.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q18', 'q': 'Which number card makes this equation true: 9 + ___ = 17?', 'options': ['8', '7', '9', '6'], 'ans': '8', 'hint': 'Calculate 17 - 9.', 'explanation': '17 - 9 = 8, so 9 + 8 = 17.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Unknown addend: 9 + ? = 17.', 'step2': 'Calculate 17 - 9 = 8.', 'step3': 'Check: 9 + 8 = 17.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q19', 'q': 'A runner has completed 28 laps and aims for 50 laps. How many laps remain?', 'options': ['22 laps', '32 laps', '18 laps', '24 laps'], 'ans': '22 laps', 'hint': 'Jump 2 to reach 30, then 20 to reach 50.', 'explanation': '50 - 28 = 22 laps.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Goal = 50, completed = 28.', 'step2': 'From 28, jump +2 to 30, then +20 to 50. Total jump = 2 + 20 = 22.', 'step3': 'Verify: 28 + 22 = 50 laps.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q20', 'q': 'On a ginladi, starting at bead 18 and jumping forward 10 lands on which bead?', 'options': ['28', '27', '29', '38'], 'ans': '28', 'hint': 'A leap of 10 increases the tens digit by 1.', 'explanation': '18 + 10 = 28.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start = 18.', 'step2': 'Add 10: 18 + 10 = 28.', 'step3': 'Bead 28 is the landing point.'}}]}, {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'badge': 'Mastery Quiz 3', 'sim_id': 'quiz_challenge_3', 'description': 'Test your competence on backward line jumps, decade leaps, estimation, and 100-grid arrow navigation.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c4_q21', 'q': 'Out of 52 total stamps, 37 are portrait stamps and the rest are monument stamps. How many monument stamps are there?', 'options': ['15 stamps', '17 stamps', '25 stamps', '14 stamps'], 'ans': '15 stamps', 'hint': 'Subtract 37 from 52 using backward jumps.', 'explanation': '52 - 37 = 15 monument stamps.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total stamps = 52, portraits = 37.', 'step2': 'Jump back 30 from 52 to 22. Then jump back 7 from 22 to 15.', 'step3': 'Total monument stamps = 15.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q22', 'q': 'How many more stamps are needed to reach a target of 75 if you currently have 52 stamps?', 'options': ['23 stamps', '25 stamps', '22 stamps', '33 stamps'], 'ans': '23 stamps', 'hint': 'Find 75 - 52.', 'explanation': '75 - 52 = 23 stamps needed.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Target = 75, current = 52.', 'step2': 'Jump from 52 to 72 (+20), then from 72 to 75 (+3).', 'step3': 'Total stamps needed = 20 + 3 = 23.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q23', 'q': 'A grasshopper hops backward from 67 by two jumps of 10. Where does it land?', 'options': ['47', '57', '37', '45'], 'ans': '47', 'hint': '67 - 10 = 57, and 57 - 10 = 47.', 'explanation': '67 - 20 = 47.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 67.', 'step2': 'First backward leap of 10: 67 - 10 = 57.', 'step3': 'Second backward leap of 10: 57 - 10 = 47.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q24', 'q': 'A foraging team has 60 ants in total. If 42 are marching outside, how many are hidden under the leaf?', 'options': ['18 ants', '28 ants', '16 ants', '22 ants'], 'ans': '18 ants', 'hint': 'Calculate 60 - 42.', 'explanation': '60 - 42 = 18 ants.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total ants = 60, visible = 42.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 60 - 40 = 20, and 20 - 2 = 18.', 'step3': 'Hidden ants = 18.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q25', 'q': 'On a 100-grid, what does moving one step straight UP represent?', 'options': ['Subtract 10 (-10)', 'Add 10 (+10)', 'Subtract 1 (-1)', 'Divide by 10'], 'ans': 'Subtract 10 (-10)', 'hint': 'The row above contains numbers 10 less.', 'explanation': 'Moving one cell up on a 100-grid subtracts 10.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Each row in a 100-grid represents a decade of 10 numbers.', 'step2': 'Moving up shifts to the previous row.', 'step3': 'Conclude moving up is equivalent to subtracting 10.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q26', 'q': 'Starting at 38 on a 100-grid, follow the path [Down, Down, Right]. On which number do you land?', 'options': ['59', '58', '68', '49'], 'ans': '59', 'hint': 'Down adds 10, Down adds 10, Right adds 1.', 'explanation': '38 + 10 = 48, 48 + 10 = 58, 58 + 1 = 59.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 38.', 'step2': 'First Down: 38 + 10 = 48. Second Down: 48 + 10 = 58.', 'step3': 'Right: 58 + 1 = 59.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q27', 'q': 'Starting at 73 on a 100-grid, follow [Up, Left, Left]. What is your final position?', 'options': ['61', '62', '63', '51'], 'ans': '61', 'hint': 'Up is -10 (63), then Left is -1 (62), Left is -1 (61).', 'explanation': '73 - 10 = 63, 63 - 1 = 62, 62 - 1 = 61.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 73.', 'step2': 'Up: 73 - 10 = 63.', 'step3': 'Left, Left: 63 - 2 = 61.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q28', 'q': 'In the Grid Race Game, if a player on cell 58 rolls digits 1 and 4, which forward move is possible without exceeding 100?', 'options': ['Both adding 14 (to 72) and adding 41 (to 99)', 'Only adding 14, because 41 goes over 100', 'Only adding 41', 'Neither move is allowed'], 'ans': 'Both adding 14 (to 72) and adding 41 (to 99)', 'hint': 'Check 58 + 14 and 58 + 41.', 'explanation': '58 + 14 = 72 and 58 + 41 = 99; both are within the 1-100 board.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Option A: 58 + 14 = 72 (valid).', 'step2': 'Option B: 58 + 41 = 99 (valid, enters 91-100 winning zone).', 'step3': 'Both moves are valid.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q29', 'q': 'Before calculating 89 - 31, which is the best quick mental estimate?', 'options': ['Around 60 (since 90 - 30 = 60)', 'Around 20', 'Around 100', 'Around 40'], 'ans': 'Around 60 (since 90 - 30 = 60)', 'hint': 'Round 89 to 90 and 31 to 30.', 'explanation': 'Rounding to nearest tens gives 90 - 30 = 60 (exact is 58).', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Round 89 to nearest ten = 90.', 'step2': 'Round 31 to nearest ten = 30.', 'step3': 'Estimated difference: 90 - 30 = 60.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q30', 'q': 'What is 64 - 28 calculated using open number line jumps?', 'options': ['36', '38', '34', '46'], 'ans': '36', 'hint': '64 - 20 = 44, then 44 - 8 = 36.', 'explanation': '64 - 28 = 36.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Start at 64.', 'step2': 'Jump back 20: 64 - 20 = 44.', 'step3': 'Jump back 8: 44 - 4 = 40, and 40 - 4 = 36.'}}]}, {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'badge': 'Mastery Quiz 4', 'sim_id': 'quiz_challenge_4', 'description': 'Test your mastery on magic squares, harvest additions with regrouping, and 100-complements.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c4_q31', 'q': 'In a 3x3 magic square with sum 15, the top row has 8 and 1. What is the missing third number?', 'options': ['6', '5', '7', '4'], 'ans': '6', 'hint': '8 + 1 + ? = 15.', 'explanation': '8 + 1 = 9, and 15 - 9 = 6.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Top row sum must be 15.', 'step2': 'Known numbers: 8 + 1 = 9.', 'step3': 'Missing number: 15 - 9 = 6.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q32', 'q': 'In the same magic square, if the middle row has 3 and 7, which central number completes the sum of 15?', 'options': ['5', '4', '6', '8'], 'ans': '5', 'hint': '3 + ? + 7 = 15.', 'explanation': '3 + 7 = 10, and 15 - 10 = 5.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Middle row numbers: 3, center, 7.', 'step2': '3 + 7 = 10.', 'step3': '15 - 10 = 5 is the central anchor.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q33', 'q': 'A farmer harvests 25 red radishes and 36 white radishes. What is the total harvest count?', 'options': ['61 radishes', '51 radishes', '60 radishes', '71 radishes'], 'ans': '61 radishes', 'hint': 'Add tens: 20 + 30 = 50. Add ones: 5 + 6 = 11. Combine 50 + 11.', 'explanation': '25 + 36 = 61 radishes.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Tens: 20 + 30 = 50.', 'step2': 'Ones: 5 + 6 = 11 (1 ten and 1 one).', 'step3': 'Total = 50 + 11 = 61 radishes.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q34', 'q': 'A wooden crate holds 100 vegetables. If 65 are tomatoes, how many carrots fill the crate completely?', 'options': ['35 carrots', '45 carrots', '25 carrots', '30 carrots'], 'ans': '35 carrots', 'hint': 'Find the complement: 100 - 65.', 'explanation': '100 - 65 = 35 carrots.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total capacity = 100, tomatoes = 65.', 'step2': 'From 65, jump +5 to 70, then +30 to 100.', 'step3': 'Carrots needed = 5 + 30 = 35.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q35', 'q': 'Two orchard baskets contain 85 apples and 67 apples. What is the grand combined total?', 'options': ['152 apples', '142 apples', '162 apples', '150 apples'], 'ans': '152 apples', 'hint': 'Add tens: 80 + 60 = 140. Add ones: 5 + 7 = 12.', 'explanation': '85 + 67 = 152 apples.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': '80 + 60 = 140.', 'step2': '5 + 7 = 12.', 'step3': '140 + 12 = 152 apples.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q36', 'q': 'By how many apples does the first basket of 85 exceed the second basket of 67?', 'options': ['18 apples', '16 apples', '22 apples', '12 apples'], 'ans': '18 apples', 'hint': 'Find the margin difference: 85 - 67.', 'explanation': '85 - 67 = 18 apples.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Basket 1 = 85, Basket 2 = 67.', 'step2': 'Subtract: 85 - 60 = 25, and 25 - 7 = 18.', 'step3': 'Difference margin = 18 apples.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q37', 'q': 'In a school book sale, 48 science books and 37 storybooks were sold. What was the total books sold?', 'options': ['85 books', '75 books', '84 books', '95 books'], 'ans': '85 books', 'hint': '48 + 37: 40 + 30 = 70, 8 + 7 = 15.', 'explanation': '48 + 37 = 85 books.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Tens sum: 40 + 30 = 70.', 'step2': 'Ones sum: 8 + 7 = 15.', 'step3': 'Combined total = 70 + 15 = 85 books.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q38', 'q': 'In a friendly cricket match, the opening team scored 76 runs in the first innings and 58 in the second. What was their total score?', 'options': ['134 runs', '124 runs', '144 runs', '132 runs'], 'ans': '134 runs', 'hint': '76 + 58: 70 + 50 = 120, 6 + 8 = 14.', 'explanation': '76 + 58 = 134 runs.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Innings 1 = 76, Innings 2 = 58.', 'step2': '70 + 50 = 120, and 6 + 8 = 14.', 'step3': 'Total score = 120 + 14 = 134 runs.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q39', 'q': 'A school canteen prepared 90 hot vadas. During recess, 64 vadas were bought. How many vadas remained?', 'options': ['26 vadas', '36 vadas', '24 vadas', '34 vadas'], 'ans': '26 vadas', 'hint': 'Calculate 90 - 64.', 'explanation': '90 - 64 = 26 vadas.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total vadas = 90, sold = 64.', 'step2': '90 - 60 = 30, and 30 - 4 = 26.', 'step3': 'Remaining vadas = 26.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q40', 'q': 'A terrace garden has 43 flowering pots and 29 herbal pots. What is the total pot count?', 'options': ['72 pots', '62 pots', '71 pots', '74 pots'], 'ans': '72 pots', 'hint': '43 + 29 = 43 + 30 - 1.', 'explanation': '43 + 29 = 72 pots.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Flowering = 43, herbal = 29.', 'step2': 'Use compensation: 43 + 30 = 73, then 73 - 1 = 72.', 'step3': 'Total garden pots = 72.'}}]}]
